{
  "email": "james.oconnor@brightline.example",
  "reason": "Ticket sender reports desktop not on network and floor 2 printer down; need account and device hostnames."
}

{
  "hostname": "BL-PR-FL2",
  "reason": "Checking whether floor 2 printer is online per KB-022 quick check."
}

{
  "hostname": "BL-DT-0108",
  "reason": "Checking desktop connectivity for user reporting no network."
}

{
  "user_id": 8,
  "reason": "Checking recent sign-in and network context for desktop connectivity issue."
}

{
  "summary": "User James O'Connor (user_id 8, Warehouse Supervisor, Operations, Chicago) reports two issues. (1) Desktop BL-DT-0108 is not on the network. check_device_status shows online: false, last check-in 2026-10-11T00:59Z, disk fine (78% free), no warnings. Account is active, not blocked, no failed logins, VPN access enabled, and sign-in history is normal and in usual hours (last corporate m365 sign-in 2026-10-09, residential VPN sign-ins). No KB article covers a desktop losing network connectivity, so this needs Tier 2 investigation (network path, switch port, NIC, or whether the device is still powered on and reachable). (2) Floor 2 printer BL-PR-FL2 is offline for everyone (online: false, last check-in 2026-10-10T21:59Z, no warnings). Per KB-022 the printer is the problem, not the laptop. Still needed: someone on-site to check the printer screen for errors and power-cycle it, and confirm the network cable and link light. If it stays offline after a power cycle with no error shown, it's a possible network or hardware fault for Tier 2 (KB-022 escalation criteria). The user has been asked to do the on-site checks and report back. Ticket is P3.",
  "reason": "Desktop has no network connectivity with no matching KB article, and the floor 2 printer is offline for all users; both need deeper investigation than Tier 1 tools allow."
}

{
  "outcome": "escalated",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi James, sorry about the rough morning. I checked both issues. Your desktop (BL-DT-0108) is showing as offline and not connected to the network, and I don't have a standard fix for that, so I've passed it to our second-level technicians to look into. The floor 2 printer (BL-PR-FL2) is also offline for everyone, so the problem is on the printer, not your laptop. If someone is near it, could they check the printer screen for any error (paper jam, out of paper or toner, open door), then turn the printer off for 30 seconds and back on? Also make sure its network cable is firmly plugged in and the link light is on. If it's still offline after that, let us know and we'll follow up. In the meantime, you can print to another printer on your floor or another nearby one.",
  "internal_note": "Checked account (user_id 8): active, not blocked, no failed logins, VPN enabled, sign-in history normal and within usual hours (06:00-14:00 Chicago). Desktop BL-DT-0108 (Windows 11, assigned to user): online false, last check-in 2026-10-11T00:59Z, disk 78% free, no warnings. No KB article for desktop network loss, so escalated to Tier 2 (escalation_id 1). Printer BL-PR-FL2: online false for everyone, last check-in 2026-10-10T21:59Z, no warnings. Per KB-022 this is a printer-side issue. Asked user to have someone on site run the KB-022 fix steps (check screen, power cycle, cable and link light). Escalate to Tier 2 if still offline after the power cycle with no error shown. No fixes or approval-gated actions were queued.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-022"
  ],
  "confidence": "medium"
}
